// campaigns domain -- reusable row predicates.
//
// Both are row-specs: they bind a concept in the signature, read its payload
// fields bare, and compile into a SQL WHERE fragment. Neither is an
// authorization predicate -- authorization in this domain is the
// @rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId") tier on every concept plus the
// ownerUserId==actor.userId conjunct every query names, and both of those are
// deliberately written at the call site rather than hidden inside a spec, so
// the per-row-authz classifier can see them.
//
// What these two DO carry is the domain's one non-obvious rule: sendability.
// "May this address be mailed" is a question with four possible answers on the
// recipient row, and the answer being a single equality is exactly why it
// belongs in one place -- the day a fifth state appears (a soft bounce that
// ages out, say) the definition changes here and every caller inherits it.

/// Matches recipients that may be mailed: subscription state 'subscribed', which is the only one of
/// the four that is sendable. 'unsubscribed' is the recipient's own choice, 'bounced' and
/// 'complained' are the provider's verdict, and all three suppress.
spec recipient isSendableRecipient {
  return subscriptionStatus=="subscribed"
}

/// Matches templates an operator has marked ready to attach to a campaign. Drafts stay out of the
/// campaign editor's picker, so half-written copy cannot be scheduled by accident.
spec template isReadyTemplate {
  return status=="ready"
}

/// Matches send jobs the drain worker still has work to do on: queued (enqueued, never batched) or
/// running (batched at least once, not yet terminal). Paused / completed / failed / cancelled are
/// all states where the worker must leave the row alone, and naming the two live ones positively is
/// safer than excluding the four dead ones -- a sixth status added later defaults to "not drained"
/// rather than to "drained".
spec sendJob isDrainableSendJob {
  return status=="queued" || status=="running"
}
